WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:05.709
Behind me is HMS Defender, one of the new
generation of Royal Navy ships. It is an incredible

00:00:05.709 --> 00:00:10.839
piece of engineering, capable of taking out
a small drone at 40km distance, capable of

00:00:10.839 --> 00:00:12.929
tracking 2,000 targets at once.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:16.850
'Cos it's a destroyer, and it is meant to
defend itself and the rest of the fleet by

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:22.930
taking out incoming missiles before they even
come close. It is a masterpiece of modern

00:00:22.930 --> 00:00:25.560
warfare engineering.

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:32.070
[MUSIC: Rule Britannia]

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:35.960
But like all big ships, it has a design flaw
that you really can't get around -- as long

00:00:35.960 --> 00:00:37.480
as you're building something out of steel.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:42.150
If you take that much metal, and put it through
a big magnetic field -- like, say, the Earth's,

00:00:42.150 --> 00:00:46.600
as it sails around -- then it's going to start
picking up magnetism. It's going to start

00:00:46.600 --> 00:00:52.210
diverting compasses. It's going to start being
detected by magnetic mines, which were developed

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:57.620
during World War 2 and afterwards to home
in on any big source of magnetic signals -- like,

00:00:57.620 --> 00:01:01.260
say, a ship that's been sailing for a while
-- and detonate next to them.

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:03.910
We're talking sea mines here, by the way,
not land mines.

00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:10.210
So how do you defend against that? Well, nowadays
you use very complicated degaussing equipment

00:01:10.210 --> 00:01:15.560
on board a ship. But in the past, you used
to drag a 2,000 amp electrical cable down

00:01:15.560 --> 00:01:20.590
the side, or set up an enormous degaussing
coil that is bigger than the ship itself,

00:01:20.590 --> 00:01:24.819
and -- well, do the same thing that you used
to do to CRT monitors when you pushed a button

00:01:24.819 --> 00:01:28.270
and they went 'dong' and distorted for a while.

00:01:28.270 --> 00:01:33.329
But what if you want to get close to a mine?
What if your ship's job is to get rid of mines,

00:01:33.329 --> 00:01:37.719
and you need to get close enough to spot them
without setting them off?

00:01:37.719 --> 00:01:44.719
Docked just downriver from Tower Bridge is
this: HMS Middleton, a Hunt-class mine countermeasures

00:01:44.990 --> 00:01:49.679
vessel. It's a minesweeper, bascially, which
means it can tell how many mines are in adjacent

00:01:49.679 --> 00:01:54.229
grid squares -- no, I'm kidding. It seeks
out and destroys mines.

00:01:54.229 --> 00:01:58.749
But it's able to do that without the mines
detecting it and detonating it because this

00:01:58.749 --> 00:02:00.469
is not made of metal.

00:02:00.469 --> 00:02:06.599
This ship is made of glass-reinforced plastic.
It's able to find and destroy mines that are

00:02:06.599 --> 00:02:11.750
hunting for magnetic things because, well,
it's not made of anything magnetic. It's just

00:02:11.750 --> 00:02:12.750
a plastic boat.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:16.939
Sounds terrible when I put it like that, but
well: this class of vessel can do more damage

00:02:16.939 --> 00:02:20.420
to one specific threat
than anything else in the Royal Navy.

00:02:22.200 --> 00:02:24.200
